WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.280
A guitar string is fixed at both ends, so waves reflect and d’Alembert’s formula does not apply directly.

2
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:14.983
Fourier’s method offers another route: build the solution as a sum of simple “modes”.

3
00:00:14.983 --> 00:00:20.030
In this part we learn the method for the string and for the rod.

4
00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:26.216
We look for a solution of the form capital X of x times capital T of t.

5
00:00:26.216 --> 00:00:31.985
Substituting, the part depending on x equals the part depending on t,

6
00:00:31.985 --> 00:00:37.754
which is only possible if both equal the same constant, minus lambda.

7
00:00:37.754 --> 00:00:45.446
The problem for X is X double prime plus lambda X equals zero, with X zero at zero and at l.

8
00:00:45.696 --> 00:00:51.641
If lambda is negative or zero, only the zero solution appears.

9
00:00:51.641 --> 00:01:02.094
If lambda is positive, X is the sine of root lambda times x, and the sine of root lambda times l must vanish.

10
00:01:02.094 --> 00:01:12.642
So the eigenvalues are lambda n equals n pi over l, squared, and the eigenfunctions are sine of n pi x over l,

11
00:01:12.642 --> 00:01:16.574
for n equal to one, two, three and so on.

12
00:01:16.824 --> 00:01:22.792
Example: u t t equals u x x for x from zero to pi, with fixed ends.

13
00:01:22.792 --> 00:01:31.344
The initial shape is two sine x minus sine three x, and the initial velocity is four sine two x.

14
00:01:31.344 --> 00:01:36.422
Here the modes are sine n x times cosine n t or sine n t.

15
00:01:36.672 --> 00:01:42.374
The data are already sums of sines, so no integrals are needed.

16
00:01:42.374 --> 00:01:47.894
At t equals zero compare: A one is two, A three is minus one.

17
00:01:47.894 --> 00:01:54.501
The velocity carries n times B n: two B two equals four, so B two is two.

18
00:01:54.501 --> 00:02:02.918
Answer: two sine x cosine t plus two sine two x sine two t minus sine three x cosine three t.

19
00:02:03.168 --> 00:02:07.304
For heat in a rod the problem for X is the same,

20
00:02:07.304 --> 00:02:13.164
but T now satisfies a first-order equation and decays exponentially.

21
00:02:13.164 --> 00:02:20.316
For example, if the initial temperature is five sine pi x plus two sine three pi x,

22
00:02:20.316 --> 00:02:30.398
the solution is five e to the minus pi squared t sine pi x plus two e to the minus nine pi squared t sine three pi x.

23
00:02:30.648 --> 00:02:38.753
The graph shows the temperature profile at three times: t equals zero, one hundredth and five hundredths.

24
00:02:38.753 --> 00:02:41.841
The third mode decays nine times faster,

25
00:02:41.841 --> 00:02:47.553
so the profile quickly becomes a single simple arch and then slowly sinks.

26
00:02:47.553 --> 00:02:52.262
On a string, by contrast, modes do not decay, they oscillate.

27
00:02:52.512 --> 00:03:01.322
Question: in a rod of length l with zero temperature at the ends, how does the n-th mode change in time?

28
00:03:01.322 --> 00:03:04.118
Watch the square in the exponent.
